-*- coding:utf-8 -*-

ssget-implied-selection-not-graphical — "_I" is classified with the graphical modes, but needs no graphics
===========================================================================================================

Reported from: SCHMS+ CAD test suite (sncf-reseau/src/schms,
tests/test-cad.lsp) run headless under `clautolisp --host mock`.
clautolisp master (post `pi` fix), 2026-08-06.

Summary
-------

`selection-and-snapshot-parity.issue` lists `"_I"` among the modes that
are out of scope for a headless engine:

    - Interactive ssget: no-mode (interactive pick), point/window forms, and
      the graphical modes "_C" "_I" "_W" "_CP" "_WP" "_F" "L" "P" "A" —
      signal a catchable AutoLISP error …

That grouping is wrong for `"_I"`, and only for `"_I"`. Every other mode
in that list needs a pointing device or a geometric hit test: `_C` and
`_W` need a window, `_F` a fence, `L`/`P`/`A` a picking history. `"_I"`
needs none of that. It means **"give me the selection that already
exists"** — the implied, or pickfirst, set. No pointer, no geometry, no
interaction.

And the machinery it needs is *already there*, two lines below in the
same list:

    - sssetfirst/ssgetfirst grip highlighting: modelled as plain pickfirst
      storage, no graphics.

So MockHost already stores a pickfirst set. `(ssget "_I")` is the read
side of that same storage, and is currently the only way to observe it.

Symptom
-------

    ERROR [sigfic-cad] fixture-cote-bas-puis-haut-discrimine --
      MockHost does not yet support ssget mode "_I";
      accepted modes are "X" and "_X".

This is what now stops the whole SCHMS+ CAD suite from running headless.
It is the last blocker: `pi` was fixed, `vla-get-activedocument` resolved,
and the suite now loads the full SCHMS+ source set and gets as far as
creating entities before hitting this.

Evidence that `_I` is passive
-----------------------------

The SCHMS+ sources contrast the two cases in the same file, which is as
close to a specification as application code gets
(src-vlx/voies-identification.lsp):

    (defun schms_voie_graphique_graines_selection (/ ss …)
      (if (setq ss (ssget "_I" *schms_filtre_voies*))      ; no prompt
        …))

    (defun schms_voie_graphique_graines_selection_manuelle (/ ss …)
      (prompt "\nSelectionnez une ou plusieurs courbes de voie …>")
      (if (setq ss (ssget *schms_filtre_voies*))            ; this one prompts
        …))

The `_I` variant is the one used when the caller wants whatever the user
had already selected; the *manuelle* variant is the interactive one. Only
the second belongs in the out-of-scope list.

Requested semantics
-------------------

1. `(ssget "_I")` returns the current pickfirst set as a PICKSET, or nil
   when it is empty. It never prompts and never signals.

2. `(ssget "_I" filtre)` applies the existing filter grammar to that set,
   exactly as `"_X"` already does over the whole database. The filter
   machinery is shared; only the source of candidates differs.

3. `(sssetfirst nil js)` populates it — already modelled. `(sssetfirst
   nil nil)` clears it.

4. `PICKFIRST` is honoured: when the system variable is 0, `(ssget "_I")`
   returns nil regardless of what was stored. This is the vendor
   behaviour and applications test it.

5. The implied selection is **consumed** on read, as on the vendors: once
   a command has taken it, the pickfirst set is cleared. **Decision (pjb):
   reproduce the CAD behaviour.**

   The simpler option — never clearing — was considered and rejected. It
   would have been easier and would probably not have bothered any
   existing test, but it would put clautolisp out of parity on a point
   that applications rely on: code that reads `"_I"` twice must see the
   set the second time only if something re-selected in between. Getting
   that wrong is the kind of divergence that surfaces late, in an
   application, rather than in a test.

   The objection that a headless eval has no notion of "command" does not
   hold: clautolisp already has one — see
   `deferred-command-special-form.issue` (COMMAND special form, mock token
   queue) and `deferred-document-lifecycle-command-semantics.issue`, which
   is precisely about NEW/OPEN/SAVE/CLOSE versus the running routine. That
   notion may well need refining as more behaviour is attached to it, and
   refining it is the right response. Ignoring it is not — least of all
   here, where command boundaries are exactly what the behaviour is
   defined against.

   A vendor probe is still wanted, to pin down *when* the clearing
   happens: at command completion, at first read, or at the next
   selection. Reproduce what the probe shows.

Why it matters
--------------

It unblocks running an entire real-world CAD test corpus headless, on
Linux CI, with no CAD licence. That is the point of the mock host, and
SCHMS+ is the corpus it was sized against.

It also opens a path the application layer wants: a TUI that lists the
document's objects, lets the user choose, calls `sssetfirst`, and then
lets the *existing* code find them through `"_I"` without knowing that no
click ever happened. That is a genuine feature for headless and terminal
use, not merely a test accommodation.

Suggested tests
---------------

- `(ssget "_I")` with nothing selected returns nil.
- `(sssetfirst nil js)` then `(ssget "_I")` returns the same membership.
- `(ssget "_I" filtre)` filters that set, sharing the `"_X"` grammar tests.
- `(setvar "PICKFIRST" 0)` then `(ssget "_I")` returns nil.
- `(sssetfirst nil js)`, then two consecutive `(ssget "_I")`: the first
  returns the set, the second nil — the consume-on-read of point 5.
- A vendor probe pinning down the exact clearing boundary on BricsCAD and
  AutoCAD, to be reproduced rather than approximated.

Related
-------

`selection-and-snapshot-parity.issue` — this issue asks to move `"_I"`
out of its "Out of scope" list. The other modes in that list stay there;
this is a reclassification of one entry, not a challenge to the headless
boundary.

Resolved (clautolisp 1.7.26)
----------------------------

Implemented `(ssget "_I")` / `(ssget "I")` on the mock host
(autolisp-mock-host/source/selection-api.lisp, host-ssget): it returns
the pickfirst set stored by `sssetfirst` as a PICKSET (or nil), never
prompts and never signals. A FILTER applies the shared "_X" grammar to
that set; PICKFIRST 0 suppresses it; and the set is consumed on read (a
second `(ssget "_I")` without an intervening `sssetfirst` returns nil) —
the vendor-parity behaviour pjb chose in point 5.

`selection-and-snapshot-parity.issue` updated to move "_I" out of its
Out-of-scope list. Tests: 5 FiveAM cases in
autolisp-mock-host/tests/selection-tests.lisp (empty→nil; membership;
shared filter; PICKFIRST 0→nil; consume-on-read), suite green.

Deferred (not blocking): the vendor probe pinning the exact clearing
boundary (command completion vs first read vs next selection) on
BricsCAD/AutoCAD — we reproduce "consume at first read". If a probe
later shows a different boundary, adjust to match.
